WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.070
When Tim Burton's The Nightmare Before Christmas
premiered in October 1993, it did so with

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:08.490
surprisingly little fanfare for a film developed
by Disney.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:12.789
It was only after the film left theaters that
it began a transition from beloved cult movie

00:00:12.789 --> 00:00:14.599
to cultural phenomenon.

00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:19.510
The story of how this creepy classic came
about is just as interesting as the film itself.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:23.160
Here's the untold truth of The Nightmare Before
Christmas.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.240
It comes from a poem

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.420
Burton originally penned the movie's story
as a poem when he was working as an animator

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:33.249
for Disney, which he likened to being stuck
in a prison.

00:00:33.249 --> 00:00:37.140
Burton could never adapt to the style preferred
by Disney, which became clear to him while

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.469
working on the 1981 feature The Fox and the
Hound, saying

00:00:40.469 --> 00:00:43.879
"I couldn't draw those four-legged Disney
foxes.

00:00:43.879 --> 00:00:45.679
[...] Mine looked like roadkills."

00:00:45.679 --> 00:00:50.089
Burton left Disney but kept in touch with
another struggling animator, Henry Selick.

00:00:50.089 --> 00:00:54.870
Burton shared his haunting poem about a skeletal
figure named Jack Skellington with Selick,

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:58.300
who showed immediate interest in directing
a feature version.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:02.440
Production started in 1991, but not without
some trouble.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:07.000
Selick didn't even have a full script in the
beginning; All he had was Burton's best wishes

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.040
and a few of Danny Elfman's songs to go on.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:11.680
Disney got scared

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.500
Disney initially considered turning Burton's
holiday themed poem into a TV special or a

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:22.180
short film similar to Vincent, a 1982 short
film also based on a Burton poem, but had

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:23.180
backed out.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:28.920
The success of 1988's Beetlejuice and 1990's
Edward Scissorhands gave Burton enough negotiating

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.700
power to arrange a deal with Disney and the
project was finally greenlit, with Burton

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.270
acting as a go-between for the studio and
his director.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:42.500
But after seeing the first trailer, then-chairman/CEO
Michael Eisner decided that The Nightmare

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:45.640
Before Christmas was just "too dark" for Disney.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:49.490
The film was sent away from the kids table
to Touchstone Pictures, where Disney kept

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:53.060
content they didn't want associated with their
child-friendly brand.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:57.550
What's wrong with disembodied legs and children
with sewn-shut eyes, anyhow?

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:00.490
Isn't that basically their slogan?

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:02.990
Filming took over three years

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:07.780
Hailing from before the days of slick CGI,
the effort that went into making The Nightmare

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:09.950
Before Christmas was intense.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:14.390
The film was shot at 24 frames per second,
which meant that the characters had to be

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:18.470
re-positioned 24 times for every second of
film.

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:23.260
It took a whole week to create just a single
minute of footage, and eight camera crews

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:24.670
worked at once.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:28.510
It wasn't just a case of nudging a figurine
slightly, either.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:33.569
The characters had to be carefully disassembled
and reassembled for every frame so their facial

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:35.980
expressions matched what was happening onscreen.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:40.520
The Pumpkin King had more than 400 interchangeable
heads alone.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.060
Oogie Boogie was trouble

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:48.551
Despite being a burlap sack filled with creepy
crawlies, Nightmare's antagonist Oogie Boogie

00:02:48.551 --> 00:02:51.650
was actually the hardest character to create.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:57.000
His complex demise in the film was particularly
tricky to navigate, consisting of four shots

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.690
that each took a full month to make.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:04.250
Not only that, but the metal armature inside
the huge figure was so strong that animators

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:09.140
had to get behind it and push it into place
for each shot, so it's no wonder he's barely

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:10.430
in the film.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:12.590
"Well, well, well!

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:15.090
What have we here?

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:16.590
Santa Clause huh?

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:19.800
Oooo I'm really scared!"

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:25.069
Some viewers also felt that Ken Page's portrayal
of the villain verged on racist.

00:03:25.069 --> 00:03:29.410
Selick defended the character, insisting that
Page modeled his voice performance on Cab

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:30.430
Calloway.

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:34.590
This argument is supported by the fact that
some lines of dialogue are lifted directly

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:39.010
from the 1933 Betty Boop cartoon The Old Man
of the Mountain, starring Calloway.

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:40.790
"Whatcha gone do now?"

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:42.700
"I'm gonna do the best I can."

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:44.080
"What are you going to do?"

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.180
"I'm gonna do the best I can."

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:50.270
Composer Danny Elfman has a long history of
channelling Calloway as well, as he did in

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:51.270
the 1980 film Forbidden Zone.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:52.270
"Well son let me tell you I'm so pleased to
meet you.

00:03:52.270 --> 00:04:01.520
The boy and I have been expecting to greet
you."

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:03.140
Best friends for never

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:08.330
All of Jack Skellington's songs were composed
and sung by Oingo Boingo frontman Danny Elfman

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:09.330
himself.

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:10.980
That's Nightmare trivia 101.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:15.410
But Elfman also recorded all of the Pumpkin
King's speaking parts, too.

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:20.050
While he nailed the singing, Elfman's voice
acting just wasn't up to Selick's standards,

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:24.350
and in the end, Burton and Selick decided
that they would have Chris Sarandon re-record

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:25.590
the scenes.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:29.520
Elfman took it badly, refusing to score Burton's
next film, Ed Wood.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:32.570
Elfman still got his speaking voice in the
film, kind of.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:37.990
The world-renowned composer also voiced The
Clown with the Tear-Away Face, and Barrel,

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:40.960
the smallest of Oogie Boogie's miniature henchmen.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:43.370
"Whatever you say Jack."

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:44.780
Creator cameos

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:49.440
In the end, Elfman's voice being in the movie
just wasn't enough, and he had the animators

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:53.250
create a shrunken version of his head and
hide it inside a cello.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:57.770
Tim Burton's severed head was also supposed
to appear in the film's finale as a hockey

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:01.419
puck, but the projectile was changed to a
jack o'lantern at the last moment.

00:05:01.419 --> 00:05:05.800
Rumor has it that Disney deemed the inclusion
of their former employee's head a step too

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:09.680
far and leaned on Selick to change it.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:10.680
Doctor in disguise

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:15.430
The biggest reveal from the unearthed storyboards
was a twist ending.

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:19.430
When Oogie Boogie's sack starts to unravel,
it isn't just worms and spiders that come

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:22.820
spilling out; Doctor Finkelstein is in there
too.

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:25.090
"Yes Jack Skellington it's me!

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:31.050
Me, the man who created Sally from bits of
flesh and scraps of cloth.

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:35.080
But she loves you Jack, you oblivious twit!"

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:39.750
A defiant Finkelstein explains that he wanted
to "teach Sally a lesson" before declaring

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:43.669
his intentions to make a new creation and
disappearing through a trap door.

00:05:43.669 --> 00:05:47.720
The Doctor's gross new creation can be seen
in the film's finale.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:52.720
Why anyone would choose a wife that looked
exactly like themselves is anyone's guess.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:56.110
But those reasons are probably pretty weird.

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:57.800
Lost voices

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:02.520
Like Chris Sarandon, many of the film's voices
weren't the creators' original choices.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.890
Burton had worked with Vincent Price on two
productions prior to Nightmare, but their

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:09.490
collaboration on Nightmare had to be called
off unexpectedly.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:13.670
Price came into the studio to record the voice
of Santa, but when the veteran actor was too

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:17.759
depressed after losing his wife, they were
forced to consider alternatives.

00:06:17.759 --> 00:06:22.389
James Earl Jones had discussions with Elfman
that ended in an angry shouting match.

00:06:22.389 --> 00:06:26.280
In the end, Ed Ivory, a relative unknown,
was entrusted with the part.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:31.479
Patrick Stewart was also brought onto Nightmare
early on in its development, recording a film-length

00:06:31.479 --> 00:06:33.130
adaptation of the poem.

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:37.040
"I'm sick of the scaring, the terror, the
fright.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:41.410
I'm tired of being something that goes bump
in the night."

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:45.500
In the end, it was decided that the opening
verses would be better recited by the film's

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:50.650
Santa, and the Stewart version ended up on
the cutting room floor, DVD extras, and the

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:51.740
film's official soundtrack.

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:56.680
Horror veteran Christopher Lee also read the
full-length poem for the film, but was also

00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:58.910
ultimately cut, to his disappointment.

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:02.150
"I'm sick of the scaring, the terror, the
fright.

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:04.690
I'm tired of being something that goes bump
in the night."

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:10.479
But, as we all know, Lee is no stranger to
being stabbed in the back.

00:07:10.479 --> 00:07:11.479
Skeleton babies

00:07:11.479 --> 00:07:16.789
The last that Jack and old Sandy Claws see
of each other is when Santa flies out of sight,

00:07:16.789 --> 00:07:19.190
leaving Halloween Town under a blanket of
snow.

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:23.970
In an unused epilogue scene, however, Santa
reveals that he actually visited his old friend

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:29.389
Jack years after their holidays disastrously
crossed paths, and found him with a handful

00:07:29.389 --> 00:07:32.330
of musically inclined children by his side.

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:39.360
"So, many years later I thought I'd drop in,
and there was old Jack, still looking quite

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:45.190
thin, with four or five skeleton children
at hand playing strange little tunes in their

00:07:45.190 --> 00:07:46.710
xylophone band."

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:50.350
Those kids actually sound a lot like Oingo
Boingo, now that we think about it.

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:52.449
There's kind of a sequel… and a prequel

00:07:52.449 --> 00:07:57.610
In the mid-2000s, Capcom released a video
game called Oogie's Revenge, which takes place

00:07:57.610 --> 00:07:59.229
after the events of the film.

00:07:59.229 --> 00:08:03.580
Jack gets bored with his life again, Oogie
is sewn back together by his trio of henchmen,

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:07.860
and the burlap sack decides he wants to be
the king of all holidays, not just Halloween.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:12.330
The game ends pretty much identically to the
film, with Oogie once again defeated and Jack

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:15.630
once again re-dedicated to his true calling
of Halloween.

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:17.030
No one said it was a great story.

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:19.350
But there's a video game prequel, also!

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:24.350
The Pumpkin King, released at the same time
as the sequel, actually explains the huge

00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:26.100
gaps in the film's continuity.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:30.830
The conflict and jealousy between Jack and
Oogie is explored, as well as exactly why

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:33.209
Oogie has to live underground in his lair.

00:08:33.209 --> 00:08:37.909
Spoiler alert: it's because once again, he
lost a fight with Jack.

00:08:37.909 --> 00:08:39.690
Jack's cameos

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:44.060
Burton has declared he'll never create a real
The Nightmare Before Christmas sequel, especially

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:46.990
not one where Jack goes to Arbor Day-Town
or something…

00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:49.870
"Excuse me, is this Hanukkah Town?"

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:53.730
"No, it's the Vatican, and I'm Pope Shalom."

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:57.130
but that didn't stop Selick from returning
to the character… twice.

00:08:57.130 --> 00:09:02.520
In his 1996 adaptation of Roald Dahl's James
and the Giant Peach, the brigade of mutant

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:07.630
insects face off against Jack as a pirate,
with one even declaring, well…

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:09.300
"A Skellington?"

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:14.130
The skeletal master of horror does his job
pretty well, but that's not even his only

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:15.130
cameo.

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:20.860
Selick's 2009 stop-motion film Coraline evokes
Nightmare in a number of ways, though those

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:23.980
with a keen eye were able to spot a direct
reference.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:29.250
As Coraline's mother cracks eggs over a bowl,
Jack's grinning skull can be seen inside one

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:30.250
of the dripping yolks.

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:35.190
It's almost a literal Easter egg, but it's
so quick, it's easy to miss.

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:36.310
Others, not so much.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:40.820
"Bunny!"

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:41.990
Thanks for watching!

00:09:41.990 --> 00:09:45.050
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:47.430
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

